 ],
 "literal": "Sliced conch shell shining bangles to slip off, every day / without sleep weeping with eyes, lamenting / here in this place staying, we will escape, right there / rise now, may you live, my heart! In front of / wild basil chaplet Vadugar's frontier / strong-speared Kaṭṭi's good country beyond / where language changes, those of the land even though they are / following [him] I have planned, in his country indeed!",
 "translation": "My shining bangles, carved from conch shells, slip off my arms day by day. Lamenting with sleepless, tear-filled eyes, we shall escape from staying here in this place. Rise now, my heart, and may you prosper! Beyond the good country of Kaṭṭi with his strong spear, past the frontier of the Vadugar people who wear chaplets of wild basil, lies a land where the language changes. Even so, I have resolved to follow him to his country!",
 "modern": "சங்கு வளையல்கள் கழன்று விழும்படியாக, நாள்தோறும் தூக்கமில்லாமல் அழும் கண்களோடு வருந்தி, இவ்விடத்தில் தங்கி வாழ்வதிலிருந்து நாம் தப்பிப்போம். என் நெஞ்சே! நீ வாழ்க! எழுவாயாக! காட்டுத்துளசி மாலையை அணிந்த வடுகர்களின் எல்லைக்கு அப்பால், வலிமையான வேலை ஏந்திய கட்டி என்பவனுடைய நல்ல நாட்டிற்கும் அப்பால், மொழி வேறுபடும் தேசமாக இருந்தாலும், தலைவனுடைய அந்த நாட்டிற்கே அவரைப் பின்தொடர்ந்து செல்ல நான் துணிந்துவிட்டேன்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her own heart in the desert landscape (pālai tiṇai). Her lover has departed to a distant land to earn wealth, and she is suffering from the separation, causing her conch-shell bangles to slip off her thinning arms. Unable to bear the loneliness and sleeplessness of staying behind, she addresses her heart, urging it to rise and accompany her. She declares her resolve to follow her lover, even though his journey has taken him beyond the frontier of the Vadugar people (who wear wild basil) and past the territory of the chieftain Kaṭṭi, into a distant land where a different language is spoken. The imagery of the distant, foreign land highlights the arduousness of the journey and the depth of her love. Key words include 'ñekiḻa' (to slip), 'vaṭukar' (Vadugar people), and 'moḻipeyar tēem' (land of a different language).",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
